  "worked_solution": "First, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2} & =37{v}_{1}+37{v}_{2}+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =37({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =37(0)+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =2{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2})=T(2{v}_{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2{v}_{2}) & =2T({v}_{2}) \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ 17 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ 34 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ 34 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}].$",
